Good catch 👍 — this is a classic circular reference issue in Power Apps.


---

❗ WHY THIS ERROR HAPPENS

You likely did something like:

HeaderContainer.Width = Gallery_Suppliers_New.Width
Gallery_Suppliers_New.Width = HeaderContainer.Width

👉 This creates a loop:

Header → depends on Gallery  
Gallery → depends on Header  
❌ Infinite loop → ERROR


---

✅ SOLUTION: USE ONE SOURCE OF TRUTH

👉 Only ONE control computes the width
👉 Others just follow it


---

🎯 RECOMMENDED FIX (CLEAN & SAFE)


---

🧩 STEP 1: Create a Variable for Total Width

Instead of referencing controls, calculate width once.

In Screen → OnVisible:

Set(
    varTotalWidth,
    150 + 120 + (CountRows('SE Question List') * 110)
)


---

🧩 STEP 2: Use Variable Everywhere

HeaderContainer:

Width = varTotalWidth


---

Gallery_Suppliers:

Width = varTotalWidth


---

👉 Now NO circular reference ✅


---

🧠 WHY THIS WORKS

varTotalWidth = fixed calculation

Header → uses varTotalWidth  
Gallery → uses varTotalWidth  
(no dependency between controls)


---

⚠️ IMPORTANT (Dynamic per Section)

If questions change per section, update variable:

Dropdown_Section → OnChange:

Set(
    varTotalWidth,
    150 + 120 +
    CountRows(
        Filter(
            'SE Question List',
            Section.Id = Dropdown_Section.Selected.ID
        )
    ) * 110
)


---

🧩 OPTIONAL (NO VARIABLE METHOD)

If you don’t want variables, use SAME formula in both:

150 + 120 +
CountRows(
    Filter(
        'SE Question List',
        Section.Id = Dropdown_Section.Selected.ID
    )
) * 110

👉 But DO NOT reference each other


---

🚨 COMMON WRONG PATTERNS

❌ This:

Header.Width = Gallery.Width
Gallery.Width = Header.Width

❌ Or:

Width = Parent.Width   // when parent depends on child


---

🖥️ FINAL RESULT

✔ No circular reference error
✔ Header and rows perfectly aligned
✔ Dynamic width per section
✔ Single scroll works properly


---

🔥 PRO TIP

If you want extra safe setup:

👉 Use variable + fixed column widths

ColumnWidth = 110


